# MalloySim scenario: an oriented box dropped onto a ground plane in 2D (M37).
#
# The 2D rigid domain has always collided its bodies as discs. M36 added a box
# collider and box-against-box collision; M37 lets that box rest on a ground
# plane (a Halfplane), the 2D sibling of the 3D box on a plane (M30). A box
# against a plane needs no separating-axis search: the plane has one normal, so
# the box is inside the solid exactly where its corners are, and the four corners
# tested against that normal are the whole story. Up to two touch at once (a flat
# edge), so the contact is a manifold, resolved as a SINGLE contact at the
# CENTROID of the penetrating corners: for a flat edge that centroid sits
# directly below the centre of mass, so the normal impulse passes through it and
# applies NO torque.
#
# That is the invariant to watch. A unit square is dropped flat, with no spin,
# straight down onto the floor. It must land flat and stay flat: its angular
# momentum stays exactly zero through the bounce, because a symmetric landing
# exerts no net torque, and it never drifts sideways. A per-corner solver that
# resolved the two corners one at a time would break the symmetry and spin the
# square up out of nothing; this one does not.
#
# The square's inertia is computed from the box and the mass, not typed in. It
# falls, bounces with restitution 0.5 losing energy each time, and settles
# resting on the floor with its centre at half a side height (y = 0.5).

type rigid

dt 0.002
steps 2000
output_every 200

# A gentle gravity and a floor at y = 0, its normal pointing up out of the solid.
gravity 0.0 -4.0
restitution 0.5
ground 0.0 1.0 0.0

# rigid_box <mass> <hx hy> <px py> <angle> <vx vy> <omega>
#
# A unit square (half-widths 0.5), centre of mass starting at y = 2, unrotated,
# at rest. It falls, its bottom edge reaching the floor when the centre is at
# y = 0.5.
rigid_box 1.0  0.5 0.5  0.0 2.0  0.0  0.0 0.0  0.0

# Expected, from a real run of the shipped binary:
#
#   step      0   momentum_x 0.00000000e+00   angular 0.00000000e+00
#   step   2000   momentum_x 0.00000000e+00   angular 0.00000000e+00
#
# The invariants:
#
# 1. Angular momentum is zero at every step. In free fall there is no torque; at
#    the landing the centroid contact sits below the centre of mass, so the
#    normal impulse makes none either. A flat drop stays flat. This is the
#    headline: the manifold is resolved without a spurious torque.
#
# 2. There is no horizontal motion. The drop is symmetric, so momentum along x is
#    exactly zero throughout.
#
# On the ASCII view (an orthographic view of the plane): a single point on the y
# axis that descends and halts, since the square's centre only moves along y.

# Machine-checkable assertions, evaluated against a real run by the scenario
# tests. `step 0` is the state before any step, and the tolerance is absolute.
# The rigid `energy` quantity adds the gravitational potential and is near the
# resting value, so the telling invariants are the two that must be exactly zero:
# no horizontal motion and no rotation, both held through the whole drop and
# bounce, which is what says the symmetric landing exerts no spurious torque.
# check step 0    momentum_x  0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 0    angular     0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 2000 momentum_x  0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 2000 angular     0.00000000e+00   tol 1e-9
